e","יָ֤גֵ֥ל",null,"H1523","HVqi3ms"],["he","לִבִּ֗י",null,"H3820","HNcmsc/Sp1cs"],["he","בִּֽישׁוּעָ֫תֶ֥ךָ",null,"H3444","HR/Ncfsc/Sp2ms","b"],["he","אָשִׁ֥ירָה",null,"H7891","HVqh1cs"],["he","לַיהוָ֑ה",null,"H3068","HR/Np","l"],["he","כִּ֖י",null,"H3588","HC"],["he","גָמַ֣ל",null,"H1580","HVqp3ms"],["he","עָלָֽי",null,"H5921","HR/Sp1cs"]]},"gl":{"H5329":"to conduct","H4210":"melody","H1732":"David","H5704":"till","H575":"where?","H3068":"LORD","H7911":"to forget","H5331":"perpetuity","H5641":"to hide","H853":"[Obj.]","H6440":"before","H4480":"from","H7896":"make","H6098":"counsel","H5315":"soul","H3015":"sorrow","H3824":"heart","H3119":"by day","H7311":"to exalt","H341":"enemy","H5921":"upon","H5027":"to look","H6030":"to dwell","H430":"God","H215":"to light","H5869":"eye","H6435":"lest","H3462":"to sleep","H4194":"death","H559":"to say","H3201":"be able","H6862":"narrow","H1523":"to rejoice","H3588":"for","H4131":"to shake","H589":"I","H2617":"kindness","H982":"to trust","H3820":"heart","H3444":"salvation","H7891":"to sing","H1580":"to wean"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Me? These expressions are figurative. God seems displeased: but it is often for our greater good. Some explain this of David, Ezechias, the captives, &c. It may be applied to every afflicted soul, which places her whole trust in God. (Calmet)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Day; frequently. (Worthington) — Septuagint adds, “and night.” (Calmet) — These cares and perplexities arise from persecutions and from man’s weakness. (Worthington)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Enemy; Saul, &c., or the devil. (St. Augustine) (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Death, by mortal sin, (Worthington) or through excessive sorrow, Jeremias li. 39. Shew me thy favour, (Calmet) and I shall be secure. Sleep is represented as nearly related to death. (Homer, Iliad xiv.)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Mercy. Man must attribute nothing to himself, otherwise he will be moved by pride. (St. Augustine) (Calmet) — Things; patience and reward. (Worthington) — The prophet feels a secret confidence arising in his breast, in consequence of God’s protection. — Yea, &c., is not in Hebrew but it is in the Septuagint, Arabic, and the ancient Fathers. Hebrew, “My heart shall be transported in thy salvation; I will praise the Lord, because he has rendered me the like,” as I placed my hopes in him: (Calmet) or, “he hath rewarded me.” The blessed Mary adopts the language of this verse, [Luke i. 46-55] and the prophet probably had the same salvation, Christ, in view. (Berthier)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}